{"case": {"id": "hotpot-5a877e5d5542993e715abf7d", "dataset": "hotpotqa", "language": "en", "scenario": "multihop_distractors", "group_id": "hotpot-5a877e5d5542993e715abf7d", "split": "dev", "question": "What screenwriter with credits for "Evolution" co-wrote a film starring Nicolas Cage and Téa Leoni?", "reference_answer": "David Weissman", "expected_behavior": "answer", "document_ids": ["hp-05706361ae64dfb1", "hp-d927cb347c8fd330", "hp-67c7933d76e6cca6", "hp-09ee8793174a7648", "hp-d1484b0338e3ea5f", "hp-79f52f9232e9f5b4", "hp-008c2cb95e690564", "hp-8d3943446390beb1", "hp-1d0722d79faa97da", "hp-6005170c96bdbc93"], "evidence": [{"document_id": "hp-09ee8793174a7648", "start": 0, "end": 46, "text": "David Weissman is a screenwriter and director."}, {"document_id": "hp-09ee8793174a7648", "start": 46, "end": 145, "text": " His film credits include "The Family Man" (2000), "Evolution" (2001), and ""When in Rome"" (2010)."}, {"document_id": "hp-6005170c96bdbc93", "start": 0, "end": 172, "text": "The Family Man is a 2000 American romantic comedy-drama film directed by Brett Ratner, written by David Diamond and David Weissman, and starring Nicolas Cage and Téa Leoni."}], "required_facts": ["David Weissman"], "forbidden_facts": [], "history": [], "annotation": "inherited_supporting_facts"}, "answer": "David Weissman is a screenwriter and director whose film credits include "Evolution" (2001). [S1]

The Family Man was written by David Diamond and David Weissman. [S3]

The Family Man stars Nicolas Cage and Téa Leoni. [S3]", "retrieved": [{"chunk_id": 7, "document_id": 4, "document_key": "hp-09ee8793174a7648", "source_spans": [[0, 145]], "document_filename": "David Weissman.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "David Weissman is a screenwriter and director. His film credits include "The Family Man" (2000), "Evolution" (2001), and ""When in Rome"" (2010).", "score": 0.6909666061401367}, {"chunk_id": 8, "document_id": 5, "document_key": "hp-d1484b0338e3ea5f", "source_spans": [[0, 414]], "document_filename": "Deadfall (1993 film).txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Deadfall is a 1993 crime drama film directed by Christopher Coppola. Coppola co-wrote the script with Nick Vallelonga. The film stars Michael Biehn, Nicolas Cage, Charlie Sheen, James Coburn, and Peter Fonda. It is also the prime influence on the song 'Deadfall', written by the American hardcore punk band Snot. A prequel/sequel, "Arsenal", starring Nicolas Cage as his character Eddie King, was released in 2017.", "score": 0.6568201780319214}, {"chunk_id": 14, "document_id": 10, "document_key": "hp-6005170c96bdbc93", "source_spans": [[0, 343]], "document_filename": "The Family Man.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "The Family Man is a 2000 American romantic comedy-drama film directed by Brett Ratner, written by David Diamond and David Weissman, and starring Nicolas Cage and Téa Leoni. Cage's production company, Saturn Films, helped produce the film. The film centers on a man who sees what could have been had he made a different decision 13 years prior.", "score": 0.6526616811752319}, {"chunk_id": 2, "document_id": 1, "document_key": "hp-05706361ae64dfb1", "source_spans": [[450, 1000]], "document_filename": "Ungod.txt", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "" Stuart Zechman, who was also playing guitar for Filter at the time, took the riff and showed it to Stabbing Westward who ended up using it as well. The song "Nothing" appeared in the "Bad Boys" movie starring Will Smith, Martin Lawrence and Téa Leoni, but was not featured on the official soundtrack album. The song also accompanied the credits of the film "Johnny Mnemonic" and was included on the film's soundtrack, along with the song "Lost". "Lost", "Lies" and "Can't Happen Here" were used in the film "Mortal Kombat", but Stabbing Westward re", "score": 0.6398710012435913}, {"chunk_id": 10, "document_id": 7, "document_key": "hp-008c2cb95e690564", "source_spans": [[0, 245]], "document_filename": "Drive Angry.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Drive Angry is a 2011 American fantasy action film starring Nicolas Cage and Amber Heard, and directed by Patrick Lussier. It was released on February 25, 2011. Shot in 3-D, the film was met with a mixed reception and grossed almost $29 million.", "score": 0.6100385189056396}], "citations": [{"source_id": 1, "chunk_id": 7, "content_sha256": "15cc66b4f8aa220ee5c9ae860f2e40072e8f1e4d8a9a893054dad237fe965bdb", "document_version": 1, "document_id": 4, "document_filename": "David Weissman.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "David Weissman is a screenwriter and director. His film credits include "The Family Man" (2000), "Evolution" (2001), and ""When in Rome"" (2010).", "score": 0.690967}, {"source_id": 3, "chunk_id": 14, "content_sha256": "59b3e099da14f1325567a4d6ca0bbf366b66898ebf8bd855148ba732f0360ac5", "document_version": 1, "document_id": 10, "document_filename": "The Family Man.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "The Family Man is a 2000 American romantic comedy-drama film directed by Brett Ratner, written by David Diamond and David Weissman, and starring Nicolas Cage and Téa Leoni. Cage's production company, Saturn Films, helped produce the film. The film centers on a man who sees what could have been had he made a different decision 13 years prior.", "score": 0.652662}]}

{"case": {"id": "hotpot-5ae7e1fc55429952e35ea9cc", "dataset": "hotpotqa", "language": "en", "scenario": "multihop_distractors", "group_id": "hotpot-5ae7e1fc55429952e35ea9cc", "split": "dev", "question": "What color clothing do people of the Netherlands wear during Oranjegekte or to celebrate the national holiday Koningsdag? ", "reference_answer": "orange", "expected_behavior": "answer", "document_ids": ["hp-ec941bb404cc5e46", "hp-18e681ee6a98907c", "hp-bb30f1305c6c0c2d", "hp-52916bc644ee2ea9", "hp-939d4d80cf03f557", "hp-79c87b50ea04e7e3", "hp-17eba74725e88dc0", "hp-039f395a241faed6", "hp-bb29fa594a7114dd", "hp-f1756a09c57e6ee5"], "evidence": [{"document_id": "hp-039f395a241faed6", "start": 257, "end": 566, "text": " It manifests itself in the wearing of orange clothing such as T-shirts, caps and scarfs; lavish attention for sports and sports fans in the media; and the decoration of cars, rooms, houses, shops, and even entire streets in orange, the traditional color of the Dutch royal family, the House of Orange-Nassau."}, {"document_id": "hp-52916bc644ee2ea9", "start": 0, "end": 86, "text": "Koningsdag (] ) or King's Day is a national holiday in the Kingdom of the Netherlands."}], "required_facts": ["orange"], "forbidden_facts": [], "history": [], "annotation": "inherited_supporting_facts"}, "answer": "Oranjegekte occurs in the Netherlands during major sporting events and during Koningsdag. [S2]

Oranjegekte manifests itself in the wearing of orange clothing such as T-shirts, caps and scarfs. [S2]

Orange is the traditional color used to decorate cars, rooms, houses, shops, and even entire streets during Oranjegekte. [S2]", "retrieved": [{"chunk_id": 15, "document_id": 9, "document_key": "hp-bb29fa594a7114dd", "source_spans": [[0, 500]], "document_filename": "White Sunday.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "White Sunday is a national holiday in Samoa falling on the second Sunday of October, with the Monday following a public holiday. It is a day for parents and communities to acknowledge and celebrate childhood by hosting special programs during church services which include scriptural recitations. Biblical story reenactments, and creative dance performances. Children receive gifts (often new clothing and/or school supplies) on White Sunday and are allowed privileges normally reserved for elders, s", "score": 0.6384543776512146}, {"chunk_id": 13, "document_id": 8, "document_key": "hp-039f395a241faed6", "source_spans": [[0, 500]], "document_filename": "Oranjegekte.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Oranjegekte (Orange craze) or Oranjekoorts (Orange fever) is a phenomenon in the Netherlands that occurs during major sporting events, especially international football championships, and during Koningsdag, an annual holiday celebrating the king's birthday. It manifests itself in the wearing of orange clothing such as T-shirts, caps and scarfs; lavish attention for sports and sports fans in the media; and the decoration of cars, rooms, houses, shops, and even entire streets in orange, the tradit", "score": 0.6269862651824951}, {"chunk_id": 18, "document_id": 10, "document_key": "hp-f1756a09c57e6ee5", "source_spans": [[450, 727]], "document_filename": "Christmas in the Basque Country.txt", "chunk_index": 1, "content": " casera outfits are normally dark blue, but can come in many colors. They wear caseras because that is what the people of the mountain wear and the holiday used to celebrate the peasants who sold their goods in town and came on Santo Tomas to pay rent to landlords in the city.", "score": 0.6198440790176392}, {"chunk_id": 8, "document_id": 5, "document_key": "hp-939d4d80cf03f557", "source_spans": [[950, 1422]], "document_filename": "Jounen Kwéyòl.txt", "chunk_index": 2, "content": " vocals are the Tambos (drums) and are beaten throughout as people speak in the creole language and have a merry time. Most people commemorate this day by wearing the island's National Wear such as the Wob Dwiete. Persons who do not want to wear the extreme layers of skirts and dresses make clothing out of special plaid material called Madras which is one of the cloths used in the national wear. Among the places which celebrate it every year are Mon Repos and Dennery.", "score": 0.6138700842857361}, {"chunk_id": 1, "document_id": 1, "document_key": "hp-ec941bb404cc5e46", "source_spans": [[0, 246]], "document_filename": "Queen's Day (disambiguation).txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Queen's Day or "Koninginnedag" is the former name of a national holiday in the Netherlands, the Netherlands Antilles and Aruba from 1890 to 2013. It has since been renamed King's Day or "Koningsdag" to honor the birthday of King Willem-Alexander.", "score": 0.6056756377220154}], "citations": [{"source_id": 2, "chunk_id": 13, "content_sha256": "1788d8d771169be8a7a2529a2713e7a59e8238e82c4712feef33cbb9c73cae5d", "document_version": 1, "document_id": 8, "document_filename": "Oranjegekte.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Oranjegekte (Orange craze) or Oranjekoorts (Orange fever) is a phenomenon in the Netherlands that occurs during major sporting events, especially international football championships, and during Koningsdag, an annual holiday celebrating the king's birthday. It manifests itself in the wearing of orange clothing such as T-shirts, caps and scarfs; lavish attention for sports and sports fans in the media; and the decoration of cars, rooms, houses, shops, and even entire streets in orange, the tradit", "score": 0.626986}]}

{"case": {"id": "hotpot-5ab3b0bf5542992ade7c6e39", "dataset": "hotpotqa", "language": "en", "scenario": "multihop_distractors", "group_id": "hotpot-5ab3b0bf5542992ade7c6e39", "split": "dev", "question": "What year did Guns N Roses perform a promo for a movie starring Arnold Schwarzenegger as a former New York Police detective?", "reference_answer": "1999", "expected_behavior": "answer", "document_ids": ["hp-d7f6c424de6dad5a", "hp-b779742539b532d2", "hp-d52fd394bfa6be7e", "hp-447e605a5ba2d57b", "hp-8fd8dc986317fa81", "hp-c73e303440055a54", "hp-a397f2f3b86351d0", "hp-32218aae1f360dea", "hp-796fc2dbb7d44559", "hp-8fa4b5f745b5c18e"], "evidence": [{"document_id": "hp-796fc2dbb7d44559", "start": 0, "end": 100, "text": ""Oh My God" is a song by Guns N' Roses released in 1999 on the soundtrack to the film "End of Days"."}, {"document_id": "hp-796fc2dbb7d44559", "start": 100, "end": 201, "text": " The song was sent out to radio stations in November 1999 as a promo for the soundtrack and the band."}, {"document_id": "hp-32218aae1f360dea", "start": 209, "end": 517, "text": " The film follows former New York Police Department detective Jericho Cane (Schwarzenegger) after he saves a banker (Byrne) from an assassin, finds himself embroiled in a religious conflict, and must protect an innocent young woman (Tunney) who is chosen by evil forces to conceive the Antichrist with Satan."}], "required_facts": ["1999"], "forbidden_facts": [], "history": [], "annotation": "inherited_supporting_facts"}, "answer": "Guns N' Roses released the song "Oh My God" in 1999 on the soundtrack to the film End of Days. [S4]

The song was sent to radio stations in November 1999 as a promo for the soundtrack and the band. [S4]

End of Days stars Arnold Schwarzenegger as former New York Police Department detective Jericho Cane. [S1]", "retrieved": [{"chunk_id": 14, "document_id": 8, "document_key": "hp-32218aae1f360dea", "source_spans": [[0, 500]], "document_filename": "End of Days (film).txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "End of Days is a 1999 American fantasy action horror thriller film directed by Peter Hyams and starring Arnold Schwarzenegger, Gabriel Byrne, Robin Tunney, Kevin Pollak, Rod Steiger, CCH Pounder, and Udo Kier. The film follows former New York Police Department detective Jericho Cane (Schwarzenegger) after he saves a banker (Byrne) from an assassin, finds himself embroiled in a religious conflict, and must protect an innocent young woman (Tunney) who is chosen by evil forces to conceive the Antic", "score": 0.6571991443634033}, {"chunk_id": 17, "document_id": 10, "document_key": "hp-8fa4b5f745b5c18e", "source_spans": [[0, 403]], "document_filename": "Get Christie Love!.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Get Christie Love! is a 1974 made-for-television film and subsequent crime drama TV series starring Teresa Graves as an undercover female police detective who is determined to overthrow a drug ring. This film is based on Dorothy Uhnak's crime-thriller novel "The Ledger". However, the main character "Christie Opara"—a white, New York Police detective—was dropped completely and "Christie Love" emerged.", "score": 0.6181821823120117}, {"chunk_id": 12, "document_id": 7, "document_key": "hp-a397f2f3b86351d0", "source_spans": [[0, 500]], "document_filename": "Last Action Hero.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Last Action Hero is a 1993 American fantasy action comedy film directed and produced by John McTiernan. It is a satire of the action genre and associated clichés, containing several parodies of action films in the form of films within the film.The film stars Arnold Schwarzenegger as Jack Slater, a Los Angeles police detective within the "Jack Slater" action film franchise. Austin O'Brien co-stars as a boy magically transported into the "Slater" universe. Schwarzenegger also served as the film's ", "score": 0.6009856462478638}, {"chunk_id": 16, "document_id": 9, "document_key": "hp-796fc2dbb7d44559", "source_spans": [[0, 334]], "document_filename": "Oh My God (Guns N' Roses song).txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": ""Oh My God" is a song by Guns N' Roses released in 1999 on the soundtrack to the film "End of Days". The song was sent out to radio stations in November 1999 as a promo for the soundtrack and the band. Despite being the band's first recorded release in almost five years, it was never issued as a stand-alone single for public retail.", "score": 0.58201664686203}, {"chunk_id": 1, "document_id": 1, "document_key": "hp-d7f6c424de6dad5a", "source_spans": [[0, 469]], "document_filename": "Arnold Schwarzenegger filmography.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Arnold Schwarzenegger is an actor who has appeared in over 30 films, and has also ventured into directing and producing. He began his acting career primarily with small roles in film and television. For his first film role, he was credited as "Arnold Strong", but was credited with his birth name thereafter. He has appeared mainly in action, and comedy films. In addition to films and television, he has appeared in music videos for AC/DC, Bon Jovi, and Guns N' Roses.", "score": 0.5706480741500854}], "citations": [{"source_id": 1, "chunk_id": 14, "content_sha256": "d97349555ac296b897df8bac949264d2c8444a1abc9567abf8248c08b1cdc268", "document_version": 1, "document_id": 8, "document_filename": "End of Days (film).txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "End of Days is a 1999 American fantasy action horror thriller film directed by Peter Hyams and starring Arnold Schwarzenegger, Gabriel Byrne, Robin Tunney, Kevin Pollak, Rod Steiger, CCH Pounder, and Udo Kier. The film follows former New York Police Department detective Jericho Cane (Schwarzenegger) after he saves a banker (Byrne) from an assassin, finds himself embroiled in a religious conflict, and must protect an innocent young woman (Tunney) who is chosen by evil forces to conceive the Antic", "score": 0.657199}, {"source_id": 4, "chunk_id": 16, "content_sha256": "3f1434068f456b1dfc31ec531786c87cc244ec51e3f20aefdb4472cad0139c8f", "document_version": 1, "document_id": 9, "document_filename": "Oh My God (Guns N' Roses song).txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": ""Oh My God" is a song by Guns N' Roses released in 1999 on the soundtrack to the film "End of Days". The song was sent out to radio stations in November 1999 as a promo for the soundtrack and the band. Despite being the band's first recorded release in almost five years, it was never issued as a stand-alone single for public retail.", "score": 0.582017}]}

{"case": {"id": "hotpot-5ab859a955429934fafe6d7b", "dataset": "hotpotqa", "language": "en", "scenario": "multihop_distractors", "group_id": "hotpot-5ab859a955429934fafe6d7b", "split": "dev", "question": "Who is the writer of this song that was inspired by words on a tombstone and was the first track on the box set Back to Mono?", "reference_answer": "Phil Spector", "expected_behavior": "answer", "document_ids": ["hp-6f079f29feebf35c", "hp-82b2d27f42edcec3", "hp-70f17b9eaaed187c", "hp-605b01128a0a998e", "hp-ce970c1d23185b92", "hp-1d5a4d742125b2fd", "hp-f5b2c9f69f1bb841", "hp-2fca066ef6a78c6b", "hp-fbd51b467da755eb", "hp-4cf2de725c17e446"], "evidence": [{"document_id": "hp-70f17b9eaaed187c", "start": 0, "end": 193, "text": "Back to Mono (1958–1969) is a box set (4 compact discs or 5 vinyl LPs) compilation of the recorded work of record producer Phil Spector, through the 1960s, released in 1991 by ABKCO as #7118-2."}, {"document_id": "hp-70f17b9eaaed187c", "start": 193, "end": 324, "text": " The first track, "To Know Him Is to Love Him," released in 1958, features Spector performing as part of the group the Teddy Bears."}, {"document_id": "hp-fbd51b467da755eb", "start": 0, "end": 139, "text": ""To Know Him Is to Love Him" is a song written by Phil Spector, inspired by words on his father's tombstone, "To Know Him Was To Love Him.""}], "required_facts": ["Phil Spector"], "forbidden_facts": [], "history": [], "annotation": "inherited_supporting_facts"}, "answer": ""To Know Him Is to Love Him" was written by Phil Spector. [S1]

The song was inspired by words on his father's tombstone, "To Know Him Was To Love Him." [S1]

"To Know Him Is to Love Him" is the first track on the box set Back to Mono (1958–1969). [S2]", "retrieved": [{"chunk_id": 23, "document_id": 9, "document_key": "hp-fbd51b467da755eb", "source_spans": [[0, 500]], "document_filename": "To Know Him Is to Love Him.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": ""To Know Him Is to Love Him" is a song written by Phil Spector, inspired by words on his father's tombstone, "To Know Him Was To Love Him." It was first recorded by the only vocal group of which he was a member, the Teddy Bears. Their recording spent three weeks at No. 1 on the "Billboard" Hot 100 singles chart in 1958, while reaching No. 2 on UK's "New Musical Express" chart. Peter & Gordon and Bobby Vinton later had hits with the song, with its title and lyrics changed to "To Know You Is to Lo", "score": 0.739325225353241}, {"chunk_id": 7, "document_id": 3, "document_key": "hp-70f17b9eaaed187c", "source_spans": [[0, 500]], "document_filename": "Back to Mono (1958–1969).txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Back to Mono (1958–1969) is a box set (4 compact discs or 5 vinyl LPs) compilation of the recorded work of record producer Phil Spector, through the 1960s, released in 1991 by ABKCO as #7118-2. The first track, "To Know Him Is to Love Him," released in 1958, features Spector performing as part of the group the Teddy Bears. Initially a vinyl album-sized package, the box contained a booklet with photographs, complete song lyrics, discographical information, and a reproduction of the essay on Spect", "score": 0.6912429928779602}, {"chunk_id": 12, "document_id": 5, "document_key": "hp-ce970c1d23185b92", "source_spans": [[0, 500]], "document_filename": "Nashville Rebel (box set).txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Nashville Rebel is a box set by Waylon Jennings, released on RCA Records through Legacy Recordings in 2006. According to Allmusic's Stephen Thomas Erlewine, it is ""the first comprehensive, multi-label Waylon Jennings retrospective ever assembled", comprising ninety-two songs recorded between 1958 and 1994, with selections from the majority of the singer's recording career. The first track of the box set is the Buddy Holly-produced "Jole Blon," released in 1958, while the last is "I Do Believe,"", "score": 0.6827526688575745}, {"chunk_id": 13, "document_id": 5, "document_key": "hp-ce970c1d23185b92", "source_spans": [[450, 1000]], "document_filename": "Nashville Rebel (box set).txt", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "eleased in 1958, while the last is "I Do Believe," a song produced by Don Was that was included on The Highwaymen's 1995 release, "The Road Goes on Forever". The other material on the box set covers Jennings' career chronologically, with songs ranging from his years on RCA's roster to later compositions from his short-lived stay at Epic Records; it ignores, however, the tracks from Jennings albums released on independent labels. The majority of the singer's charting singles are included in the package, as are collaborations such as "Mamas Don't", "score": 0.6584838628768921}, {"chunk_id": 5, "document_id": 2, "document_key": "hp-82b2d27f42edcec3", "source_spans": [[0, 500]], "document_filename": "Chrome, Smoke &amp; BBQ.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Chrome, Smoke & BBQ is a box set by American blues-rock band ZZ Top, released in 2003. At the time of release, this box set was notable for using the original mixes for all of the tracks from the band's first five albums for the first time on the CD format. This box set, and the companion release "Rancho Texicano", were the only two CD releases which featured original mixes from "ZZ Top's First Album", "Rio Grande Mud", and "Tejas", aside from 1977's "The Best of ZZ Top" which features two track", "score": 0.6493822336196899}], "citations": [{"source_id": 1, "chunk_id": 23, "content_sha256": "15e80803b2c1cc2552d5733a0ae3d325ae251a7981de09e3e2afd24788f80dbf", "document_version": 1, "document_id": 9, "document_filename": "To Know Him Is to Love Him.txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": ""To Know Him Is to Love Him" is a song written by Phil Spector, inspired by words on his father's tombstone, "To Know Him Was To Love Him." It was first recorded by the only vocal group of which he was a member, the Teddy Bears. Their recording spent three weeks at No. 1 on the "Billboard" Hot 100 singles chart in 1958, while reaching No. 2 on UK's "New Musical Express" chart. Peter & Gordon and Bobby Vinton later had hits with the song, with its title and lyrics changed to "To Know You Is to Lo", "score": 0.739325}, {"source_id": 2, "chunk_id": 7, "content_sha256": "5e93de45229450ca40f5917f50194a22e3490524fa5b5a4c24f8f7d46fc9065c", "document_version": 1, "document_id": 3, "document_filename": "Back to Mono (1958–1969).txt", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Back to Mono (1958–1969) is a box set (4 compact discs or 5 vinyl LPs) compilation of the recorded work of record producer Phil Spector, through the 1960s, released in 1991 by ABKCO as #7118-2. The first track, "To Know Him Is to Love Him," released in 1958, features Spector performing as part of the group the Teddy Bears. Initially a vinyl album-sized package, the box contained a booklet with photographs, complete song lyrics, discographical information, and a reproduction of the essay on Spect", "score": 0.691243}]}

